1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:05,510
In 1936, a photographer said a woman was drifting down these stairs.

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:07,170
Raynham Hall, Norfolk.

3
00:00:07,190 --> 00:00:09,100
A Country Life photo shoot.

4
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:15,640
Legend says the Brown Lady is Dorothy Walpole, who died in 1726.

5
00:00:16,390 --> 00:00:21,650
In 1836, novelist Frederick Marryat claimed he shot at her.

6
00:00:22,230 --> 00:00:25,950
A century later, Provand and Shira aimed a camera up the stairs.

7
00:00:26,510 --> 00:00:30,100
Shira said he saw a vapoury form, slowly becoming a woman.

8
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:32,420
Provand uncapped the lens.

9
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:34,030
Shira fired the flash.

10
00:00:34,510 --> 00:00:36,540
The plate showed a pale, veiled shape.

11
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:38,550
Country Life printed it.

12
00:00:38,570 --> 00:00:40,230
So did Life magazine.

13
00:00:40,510 --> 00:00:43,250
Experts reportedly found no sign of tampering.

14
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:46,040
But sceptics see a double exposure:

15
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:47,560
two images, one plate.

16
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:49,210
Or grease on the lens.

17
00:00:49,230 --> 00:00:51,350
Or Shira moving on the stairs.

18
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:53,520
Nobody has ever proved which.

19
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,390
So look again at these stairs.
